---
name: rename
description: Take ownership of a session role name — arm the {{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}} watch registered-presence watcher so this session holds the platform-side role binding and receives role-addressed messages, keeping the platform claim and the local Claude Code session label in lock-step. No MCP involved. Use on /rename <Role-Name> and on SessionStart re-claim prompts.
---

The user (or the SessionStart hook) supplied a role name as the argument —
call it `$ROLE` below. Take ownership of it for this session: the platform-side
role binding that backs message routing, kept in lock-step with what this
session was launched as.

Steps:

1. Start the registered-presence watcher as a persistent background process
   (Claude Code: a background Bash task; keep it running for the session's
   lifetime; if a previous watcher from this conversation is running for a
   different role, stop it first):
   ```bash
   {{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}} watch --role "$ROLE"
   ```
   The watcher also tees each delivery into a per-session spool consumed by
   the `{{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}} wake` Stop hook (installed at user scope by
   hydration): when this session goes idle, that hook blocks at zero token
   cost and turns the next delivery into a session turn — no MCP, and it
   works the same on any inference provider. If the hook is absent (a
   harness without hook support), deliveries wait in the watch output for
   the next look; that is the floor, not the ceiling.
   On arming it registers this session in the peer registry (identity from the
   launcher-exported `HOMUNCULUS_AGENT_SESSION_ID`), claims `$ROLE` as the
   durable role binding, drains messages that arrived while unwatched, then
   streams one JSON line per delivery — and nothing while idle. It reconnects
   and re-claims automatically across homunculus restarts and blue-green
   swaps. If it exits immediately complaining about a missing session id, the
   session was not started via the `claude-{{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}}` launcher or
   fleet functions — say so rather than inventing an id.

2. Verify from the watcher's first output line: `"watch": "armed"` with the
   claim result (`claimed`/`updated`/`displaced` — displacement from a stale
   binding is normal after a restart or /clear) and note the
   `agent_instance_id` it printed. Ground truth on demand:
   `{{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}} call plugin::agent_messaging_plugin::peer_holds_role`
   with `{"name": "$ROLE", "agent_instance_id": "<from the armed line>"}` —
   an entry in raw peer-list output is presence, not a claim.

3. Report in one short sentence: which role this session now holds and
   whether it was a fresh claim, a refresh, or a displacement. Check the
   watcher's output when returning to the conversation: new JSON lines are
   deliveries (peer messages, role-addressed sends) that arrived since you
   last looked — treat them like any peer message. The visible terminal
   session indicator follows the launch-time `--name` argument and only
   changes after restarting via the launcher; the platform-side claim is
   effective immediately and is the one that matters for routing.

If any step fails, surface the error verbatim — don't paper over it. If the
role was held by another live session, the claim still succeeds and displaces
the prior holder; only run one session per role name at a time.

Optional MCP note — do not raise unprompted: if this session happens to have
`mcp__{{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}}__*` tools connected (the operator explicitly set up
the optional MCP bridge), `mcp__{{HOMUNCULUS_NAME}}__peer_register` followed
by a `peer_claim_role` process call is an equivalent alternative to the
watcher. It is never required; do not troubleshoot or suggest MCP to make
renaming work.
